findings.remediation:
Replace the hand-built member string with a google_project_service_identity resource, which creates the agent if it doesn't exist yet (idempotent if it does) and exposes its real email:

resource "google_project_service_identity" "cloudsql_sa" {
  provider = google-beta
  project  = data.google_project.this.project_id
  service  = "sqladmin.googleapis.com"
}

resource "google_kms_crypto_key_iam_member" "cloudsql_sa" {
  crypto_key_id = google_kms_crypto_key.sql.id
  role          = "roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter"
  member        = "serviceAccount:${google_project_service_identity.cloudsql_sa.email}"
}

Requires adding a google-beta provider entry to example/examplea/terraform.tf's required_providers (implementations, not the module itself, own required_providers here).

Separately: even with the depends_on ordering fixed earlier this session, GCP IAM grants can take up to ~60s to fully propagate — the first apply of a brand-new key + grant + instance chain can still occasionally hit a transient permission-denied on the instance creation call even though the grant object itself was created successfully. If that's observed in practice, a short time_sleep between the grant and module.cloudsql (or a documented retry-on-fail note) is the standard workaround; not proposed as a mechanical fix here since it's a timing tradeoff, not a correctness bug.
